  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nVALLEY RAIN AND MOUNTAIN SNOW THROUGH TONIGHT:\n\nSatellite imagery shows that the low pressure system that is  \nbringing widespread rain and mountain snow is currently centered\nover southwestern Colorado. The low will move across western \nColorado today and will be centered over eastern Colorado by \nThursday morning. Snowfall was observed this morning over some \nmountain passes and higher terrain areas, such as the San Juan \nMountains and the Grand Mesa, but sunrise and the subsequent \nincrease in temperatures transitioned snowfall to rainfall in \nmany of these areas. However, snowfall is still the likely \nprecipitation type at the highest elevations, and a transition \nfrom rain to snow or a rain/snow mix is expected this evening \nand tonight where precipitation occurs in the mountains.\n\nThe bulk of the total expected snowfall for the southern \nColorado mountians fell earlier today while forcing from the low\nwas more favorable in this area. However, 1 to 3 inches of\nadditional snowfall is possible over the highest terrain of the\nsouthern mountains. The central and northern mountains are \nexpected to receive 4 to 8 inches of snow along the highest \nterrain as forcing becomes more favorable in this area through \nthe remainder of the day. Elevation-based Winter Weather \nAdvisories remain in effect through 6 PM for the southern \nColorado mountains, and through 6 AM Thursday for the central \nand northern Colorado mountains. \n\nCOOL AND DRY FOR THE LATTER HALF OF THE WEEK:\n\nDrier air moves into the region after the low pressure system \nmoves east of the Rocky Mountains on Thursday morning. The \ncurrent forecast keeps precipitation chances low Thursday \nafternoon through Sunday, especially outside of the higher \nterrain areas. A much cooler airmass is left in its wake, and \ncould lead to the first hard freeze of the year across northwest\nColorado and some mountain valleys on Thursday night. \nConfidence in these overnight temperatures has increased, so a  \nFreeze Watch has been issued for Thursday night through Friday \nmorning. Overnight low temperatures warm slightly after \nThursday night, but may still fall below freezing in some areas\nof northwest Colorado and in mountain valleys. High \ntemperatures will increase daily, but will still remain below \nnormal through the weekend.",
